{
  "app": "what-would-warren-say",
  "version": 1,
  "exportedAt": "2026-10-08T12:54:58.915Z",
  "book": "Warren Buffett and the Interpretation of Financial Statements",
  "entries": {
    "1": {
      "summary": "Identify a company with a durable competitive advantage.\n\nValue a company with a durable competitive advantage.",
      "notes": "",
      "type": "background",
      "updated": "2026-10-08T10:55:45.312Z"
    },
    "2": {
      "summary": "DCA companies make the most money over the longer term",
      "notes": "",
      "type": "background",
      "updated": "2026-10-08T11:13:26.275Z"
    },
    "3": {
      "summary": "- A company that sells a unique product/service.\n(product eg. Coca-Cola, Pepsi, Hershey's, Budweiser, Coors, Kraft, Waahington post, Proctor & Gamble, Philip Morris)\n(service eg. Moody's, H & R Block Inc. AMEX, Service Master Co., Wells Fargo - Institution specific, not people specific)\n\n- Low cost buyer/seller of product/service the public consistently needs. Big margins are traded for volume as increase in volume makes up for low margins. The best price in town becomes part of the consumer's story of where to shop.\n(eg. Wal-Mart, Costco, Nebraska, Furniture Mart, Borsheim Jewelers, Burlington Northern, Santa Fe Railway",
      "notes": "",
      "type": "background",
      "updated": "2026-10-08T11:03:13.644Z"
    },
    "4": {
      "summary": "-Durability creates wealth\n(Coca-Cola has been selling the same thing for well over a century)\n\n-Consistency in the product creates consistency in the companies profits.\n\n-The company doesn't have to spend millions to keep changing the product with R&D nor on re-tooling for the next product. Less debt means less interest meaning more money to expand or buy back stock, driving up earnings and the price of the stock.\n\n-Is it consistent? - High gross margin, little to no debt, not spending heavy on R&D?\n\n-All of this info can be found in Financial Statements\n",
      "notes": "",
      "type": "overview",
      "updated": "2026-10-08T11:13:12.820Z"
    },
    "5": {
      "summary": "Income Statement - How much is earned in a set period? Determines margins - Return equity, cosistency and direction of earnings, these thing determins DSA status.\n\nBalance Sheet - How much is in the bank? How much it owes? Subtract these and you get net worth. Amount of cash and long term debt are indicators of DCA.\n\nCash Flow Statement - Cash that flows in and out. Good to see how much is spent of capital improvements. Tracks bond/stock sales & repurchases.",
      "notes": "",
      "type": "overview",
      "updated": "2026-10-08T11:17:47.457Z"
    },
    "6": {
      "summary": "Warren uses MSN, Yahoo, SEC, Bloomberg",
      "notes": "",
      "type": "background",
      "updated": "2026-10-08T11:18:10.001Z"
    },
    "7": {
      "summary": "Warren starts with the income statement.\n\nExpenses, Revenue, Profit/Loss\n\nIndicates DSA or Mediocre where the latter shows a struggle year to year in a competitive market.",
      "notes": "",
      "type": "overview",
      "updated": "2026-10-08T11:26:43.817Z"
    },
    "8": {
      "summary": "-Amount of money that comes in in a set period\n\n-Big revenue doesn't mean profitable\n\n-Revenue without expenses means nothing\n\n-After revenue, take a long careul look at the expenses",
      "notes": "",
      "type": "metric",
      "updated": "2026-10-08T11:27:55.014Z"
    },
    "9": {
      "summary": "-AKA the cost of revenue\n\n-The lower the better\n\n-Find out what is included in the cost. It gives a good idea of how management is thinking about the business.\n\n-As a lone number it doesn't tell us much about DSA.\n\n-Determines gross profit",
      "notes": "",
      "type": "metric",
      "updated": "2026-10-08T11:31:39.651Z"
    },
    "10": {
      "summary": "GP Margin = GP / Revenue\neg. 7000 / 10000 = 70%\n\nGP = Revenue - cost of raw good  and labor to make the goods\n\nDoesn't include sales & admin costs, depreciation, interest costs of running the business\n\nGP doesn't tell us much but GP margin can tell us a lot about the economic nature of a business\n\nConsistently higher GP Margins show excellent long term economics working in their favour than those that don't\n(eg. Coca-Cola shows 60% + consistently, Moody's 73%, Burlington Northern Santa Fe Railway 61%, Wrigely & Co. 51%)\n(Contrast eg. United Airlines 14% and always in & out of bankruptcy, GM 21%, US Steel 17% Goodyear 20% in a bad economy)\n\nWarren stays away from technology because he says he doesn't understand it.\nMicrosoft 79%\nApple 33%\nIndicates that Microsoft has better economics?\n\nDCA creates higher margins hence allows the freedom to price the products/services well in excess of cost of goods sold.\n\n40%+ tend to have DCA but there are exceptions\n\nLower than 40% indicates a highly competitive industry where competition hurts margins but there are exceptions\n\nBelow 20% indicates fiercely competitive industry\n\nCompanies without a competitve advantage are never going to make use rich\n\nThe GP Margin test is not failsafe but it is an early indicator that they have DCA\n\nTrack the last 10 years to ensure consistency as consistency is the name of the game\n\nhigh research costs, high sellin & admin costs and high interest on debt can strip the competitive advantage potential from a high GP margin company. They can be a thorn in the side of any company. These are called operating expenses",
      "notes": "",
      "type": "metric",
      "updated": "2026-10-08T12:54:52.456Z"
    },
    "11": {
      "summary": "A group of expenses, when added, make up the total operating expenses.\n\nThese are all the company's hard costs associated with research and development of new products, selling and administrative costs of getting the product to market, depreciation and amortization, restructuring and impairment charges, and the catch-all \"other\" that includes all non-operating, non-recurring expenses.",
      "notes": "",
      "type": "overview",
      "updated": "2026-10-07T12:54:42.580Z"
    },
